# /etc/pam.d/yubikey-auth — YubiKey step-down auth.
#
# GENERATED by zenbook/yubikey/install-yubikey-auth.sh from
# zenbook/yubikey/pam/yubikey-auth.in. Local edits here are overwritten on the
# next install — change the template or /etc/yubikey-auth/config and re-run
# the installer.
#
# A service opts in by replacing its "@include common-auth" line with
# "@include yubikey-auth".
#
#   1. window check  closed -> jump over the WHOLE YubiKey block and go
#                    straight to the password stack.
#   2. YubiKey       repeated U2F_ATTEMPTS times. pam_u2f has no retry option
#                    of its own, so the PAM idiom is to list the module more
#                    than once: each failure is ignored and falls to the next
#                    copy, giving another go at the sensor. A success ends the
#                    entire stack (success=done), so a touch never extends the
#                    window — 12h is measured from the last password.
#   3. common-auth   the stock Debian password stack.
#   4. stamp         reached only when a password actually succeeded: a wrong
#                    password aborts inside common-auth at its
#                    "auth requisite pam_deny.so" line, which returns from the
#                    whole stack before control can reach here. A correct
#                    password jumps over pam_deny to pam_permit and falls out
#                    of common-auth into this line.
#
# The "default=" count on the check line MUST equal the number of pam_u2f
# lines below it — that is how a closed window skips the whole block and lands
# exactly on common-auth. The installer keeps the two in sync from a single
# setting; do not edit one without the other.
#
# `seteuid` is required, not cosmetic. pam_exec runs the helper with the REAL
# uid unless told otherwise, and sudo is setuid-root: under it the real uid
# stays the invoking user while only the effective uid is root. Without this,
# `check` cannot read the root-owned state file and `stamp` refuses to write
# it, so sudo would sit permanently on the password branch. gdm is unaffected
# (it is already root either way), which is precisely why this must not be
# "simplified" away after testing only the login path.
#
# Every failure mode degrades to "type your password" — a closed window, an
# absent key, exhausted touch attempts, a missing mapping file, or a broken
# helper. There is no state in which this stack can lock you out.

auth  [success=ok default=@U2F_ATTEMPTS@]  pam_exec.so seteuid quiet quiet_log @HELPER@ check
@U2F_LINES@
@include common-auth
auth  optional                       pam_exec.so seteuid quiet quiet_log @HELPER@ stamp
